  {
    "episode_id": 95603,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/5/95603.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 29,
    "turn_text": "Well, the issue is For, again, lay people, judges and prosecutors and defense attorneys who, and juries, and juries, people who sit on juries, most importantly, to understand that a forensic algorithm that has been designed to do probabilistic DNA matching might have the appearance of infallibility. Because it has the patina of science around it. But what's concerning is an article by Rebecca Wexler, who's now a professor at Berkeley Law, wrote an article called, it was entitled, Convicted by Code, caught the attention of some of my staff. And then they brought the concerns to me and said, you know, boss, Now this is disturbing that the people who make or design these forensic algorithms and profit from them are selling them, probably for a good deal of money to police departments and prosecutors. that they're far from, far from perfect and we don't really know the reliability independently and objectively. And that people who are, you know, defendants in criminal cases could, you know, lose their freedom being imprisoned or even be convicted of capital crime and be punished by death. Because of the because the DNA matching evidence may be the only evidence in the case. So, you know, the stakes are quite high. And, you know, there was a case that Ms. Wuxell wrote about the Martel Chubbs case where, you know, it was in Long Beach where true allele, one of the widely used probabilistic genotype typing programs was used to identify a person named Martel Chubs as a suspect in a cold homicide case from the 1970s. The match from truly old the company was the only evidence against, was only evidence against Chubs. He requested access to the source code and he was denied. And the denial was based on the the proprietary, the intellectual property rights of true allele. So he wasn't able to challenge the evidence against them, and he eventually took a plea deal. So I think that this is a fundamental contradiction of what our justice system should be about. What I think is that intellectual property rights cannot and should not trump a defendant's ability to be able to critically review",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "A forensic algorithm designed for probabilistic DNA matching may appear infallible due to its scientific presentation.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Defendants risk losing their freedom, including facing capital punishment, based on potentially flawed DNA evidence.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "Rebecca Wexler, a professor at Berkeley Law, authored an article titled 'Convicted by Code'.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "In some criminal cases, DNA matching evidence may be the sole basis for conviction.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "Martel Chubbs requested access to the source code of the TrueAllele software but was denied.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "The developers of forensic algorithms profit financially from selling them to police departments and prosecutors.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      },
      {
        "text": "These forensic algorithms are not known to be reliable through independent and objective evaluation.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      },
      {
        "text": "In the Martel Chubbs case, TrueAllele software was used as the only evidence linking him to a 1970s cold case.",
        "confidence": 0.86
      },
      {
        "text": "The denial of source code access was justified by claiming intellectual property rights.",
        "confidence": 0.85
      },
      {
        "text": "Martel Chubbs ultimately accepted a plea deal after being unable to challenge the evidence.",
        "confidence": 0.84
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "The justice system should allow defendants to scrutinize all evidence used against them.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Intellectual property rights should not override constitutional rights to a fair trial.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "Defendants deserve full access to evidence, especially when it determines their liberty or life.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "Legal decisions based solely on opaque technology violate principles of due process.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "Scientific appearances can mislead non-experts into perceiving algorithms as infallible.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      },
      {
        "text": "Judges and juries lack sufficient expertise to assess complex forensic algorithms without transparency.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      },
      {
        "text": "Source code disclosure is essential for meaningful adversarial testing of forensic tools.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      },
      {
        "text": "Independent verification of algorithmic reliability is necessary for trustworthy legal outcomes.",
        "confidence": 0.86
      },
      {
        "text": "Profit motives in algorithm development compromise objectivity and fairness in the justice system.",
        "confidence": 0.85
      },
      {
        "text": "The use of unverifiable algorithms undermines public trust in the justice system.",
        "confidence": 0.83
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Assert / Elaborate"
