  {
    "episode_id": 95603,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/5/95603.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 32,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, it seems like basic concepts of due process should trump a particular company's proprietary rights, especially in a case like this where it's not like being able to see the source code as a defendant is going to lead to competition issues, which is sort of the intent of whatever trade secret or whatever protections you're talking about. I'm kind of amazed. I think this is important. We've written about a bunch of these stories also. I'm kind of amazed that judges haven't just made this the way things are. Anyways, that they haven't said, you know, you can't use trade secrets to hide this stuff, but that hasn't been the case, which I guess is part of what necessitates you introducing this bill.",
